      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-05-27T14:30:06Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Hi, I want to add a bit of clarification and also check my understanding.\n\nFor limited range YUV, I believe the matrices for 8,10,12,16 bit are slightly different because they also do the limited to full range scaling. And this scaling depends on the input video bit depth. E.g. for luma, limited range Y has a 219/255 scale for 8 bit and 876/1023 scale for 10 bit\n\nFor full range YUV, the matrices for 8,10,12,16 are still slightly different because the neutral U, V points is at 128/255 and 512/1023 etc.\n\nRegarding the DataFormat::kUnorm10_Unorm2. My understanding is that Nvidia samples the 10 bit video in a 16 bit texture, causing the current green tint bug and hence needing correction. While Apple GPUs sample the 10 bit video directly so is already correct? So a fix would need to use the modified 10,12 bit matrices for Nvidia, while Apple will still use the unmodified 10 bit matrix for a 10 bit video?\n\nRegarding full vs limited range. This is because video files can be full (0-1023 for Y) or limited range (64-940 for Y). This is a 10 bit video example. U and V also have their defined ranges for full and limited. I observed the green tint bug for 10 bit limited range on Nvidia, but I believe it will also occur for full range. So both the full and limited range matrices need to be modified for the cases like Nvidia, where 10, 12 bit video is sampled in a 16 bit texture.",
      "parentUuid": "770b7cf3_a6cc48e5",
      "revId": "6adbc5e5c3b7ee87dd754d66d0fddad7494deeb1",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
